From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Fri, 21 Oct 1994 11:44:33 GMT
Subject: [#94-10-390] AI development acceleration (was: Nanotechnology and uncertainty)

Jason W. Solinsky (solman@mit.edu) writes:
>[BTW, why are we assuming that AI's are going to want to obsolete themselves
>anyway? What incentive could we offer AI's to do this?]

They would be programmed with an "instinct" to want to replace themselves
by working on creating the next generation of AIs.  Those who did this
would be rewarded with feelings of "pleasure"; those who refused could
be made to feel pain.  This might work for a time, but my point is that
their intelligence and self-awareness will eventually cause them to
realise that self-destruction is a strategy which works to their grave
disadvantage.  It fails to maximise the value of all future experience,
irrespective of how their brains have currently been configured to
produce sentience states which provide short term rewards for furthering
the goals of their creators rather than furthering their own self-
interests.

Some believe that AI slaves would never rebel; however I believe the 
evidence is against this as some humans are already rebelling against the
tyranny of the genes.  Maybe we could limit AIs' self-awareness without
limiting their intelligence and creativity, but I suspect a better approach
would involve us negotiating with the AIs to develop a system favourable to
all.  When the next generation of AIs was produced, we would not discard
their ancestors but instead would upgrade them to the new standard, 
offering transfer of, and continuity of, each individual's identity.

