From: Webb_S <WebbS@oplc.psb.bls.gov>
Date: Fri, 21 Oct 94 09:19:00 EDT
Subject: [#94-10-391] URL: CSICOP/_Skeptical Inquirer_

Jason Hamilton writes:

>Newton and Einstein declared that any motion can be view from any frame
>of reference.  (Everything is relative, so to speak).  This is clearly
>NOT true in the case of rotation.  When you stir a cup of coffee, the
>surface depresses in the middle and pushes up near the edge, due to
>centrifugal force (inertia).  But, from the Coffee's frame of reference,
>it is the rest of the world that is spinning madly.  Why, then,
>does the coffee make that bowl shape?

>Ernst Mach said that it was because the Coffee was rotating within an
>INERTIAL frame of reference, perhaps created by the mass of the solar
>system.  He proposed no mechanism for this.  Furthermore, he said that
>the rotation of planets and such occured within an intertial frame of
>reference generated by the "fixed stars."  Again, no equations or
>mechanisms.  If he was right, we have no idea how this would work, or how
>strong an effect it is.  If he was wrong, than physics is sort of stuck.

I'm not familiar with Mach's principle, but I don't see anything mysterious 
about the phenomena you're describing.  In both the case of the coffee cup 
and the solar system, it is *not* true that "everything is relative" because 
both systems are *accelerating*.  The coffee is being "pushed" in a circular 
path by the walls of the cup (centrifugal force).  The planets are being 
pulled toward their axis of rotation (centripetal force).  Special 
relativity shows that it is mathematically demonstrable to show which frame 
is accelerating and which is not (thus the solution to the twin "paradox").

The coffee makes the bowl shape because the coffee molecules are trying to 
escape tangentially from the cup. but the walls are preventing this, so the 
coffee tends to push up against the edge.

Is there some subtlety here that I'm missing?

In any event, I don't see what any of this has to do with astrology...


