I distinctly, no, waitaminnit, I _vaguely_ recall a number of "mind-softening"
factors in wide circulation in the late Sixties :-)
[...]

I do recall that there was a scholarly contribution, in the form of
cultural relativity (all cultures contain elements that satisfy the
needs of those people at that level of technology, therefore no favored
platform for describing cultures) and other forms of relativism, which
were often seen as a useful antidote to the excessive rigidity of 
academics past. See Bloom, _The Closing of the American Mind_, for a
comprehensive (dare I say, "tedious"?) rant against relativism of all
sorts.

While the relativity principle is sound within the context of cultural
anthropology, it can certainly be taken out and misused.

A part of the virus coat is "least effort". Critical thinking is very
hard work, and requires that you care about tradition and precision;
letting anything pass for art, scholarship, politics, etc. is easy
by comparison, you just react to the whim of the moment.

